039886474609375, 0.0040435791015625, 0.0004887580871582031, -0.027801513671875, -0.00923919677734375, -0.004276275634765625, 0.0184783935546875, 0.006195068359375, 0.027435302734375, 0.01885986328125, -0.0225372314453125, 0.00994873046875, 0.0005593299865722656, -0.037078857421875, 0.043243408203125, -0.0084228515625, -0.0172271728515625, 0.01435089111328125, -0.01148223876953125, 0.002872467041015625, 0.0086517333984375, 0.0311737060546875, -0.0156402587890625, 0.007282257080078125, 0.0382080078125, -0.010650634765625, -0.0014629364013671875, 0.00039267539978027344, 0.01561737060546875, -0.034271240234375, -0.0068817138671875, -0.033233642578125, 0.0195770263671875, -0.01291656494140625, 0.002300262451171875, -0.01367950439453125, 0.019012451171875, -0.0253448486328125, -0.01288604736328125, -0.0450439453125, 0.002655029296875, -0.044921875, -0.0077972412109375, 0.01763916015625, -0.004241943359375, -0.0067138671875, -0.0001989603042602539], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The French Revolution (1789\u20131799) was a transformative period of political and societal upheaval in France that fundamentally reshaped the nation and influenced the world. This overview synthesizes the Revolution\u2019s causes, major phases, and lasting impacts, while acknowledging its complexities and contradictions.\n\nThe Revolution arose from a convergence of factors. Deep social inequality divided the three estates\u2014clergy, nobility, and commoners\u2014while political conflict simmered over representation and authority. Economic hardship, including a severe fiscal crisis and widespread food shortages, further fueled discontent. The convening of the Estates General in 1789, a representative assembly of all three estates, marked the beginning of open challenge to the absolute monarchy and the privileges of the elite.\n\nThe Revolution unfolded in several distinct phases. Early events such as the storming of the Bastille on 14 July 1789 symbolized popular resistance. The National Assembly\u2019s abolition of feudalism and the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen established new legal principles of equality and individual rights. The Reign of Terror (1793\u20131794), led by the Committee of Public Safety under Maximilien Robespierre, saw radical measures and mass executions in the name of defending the Revolution. Later, the Directory government (1795\u20131799), a five-member executive, attempted to stabilize France but struggled with corruption and ongoing conflict. The period concluded with the Coup of 18 Brumaire, when Napoleon Bonaparte seized power on 9 November 1799.\n\nThe Revolution\u2019s ideals\u2014such as popular sovereignty, secularism (la\u00efcit\u00e9), and citizenship\u2014were enshrined in new laws and institutions. For example, the Civil Constitution of the Clergy subordinated the church to the state, advancing secularism, while the expansion of suffrage and the abolition of noble privileges redefined citizenship. However, these advances were limited: women, enslaved people, and other marginalized groups were largely excluded from political rights, and revolutionary violence contradicted the ideals of liberty and fraternity.\n\nThe impact of the French Revolution extended far beyond France. Its principles inspired subsequent movements for democracy and national self-determination, such as the Haitian Revolution and the revolutions of 1848 across Europe. The Napoleonic Code influenced legal systems worldwide, and debates over secularism and social justice continue to shape modern French political discourse.\n\nIn sum, the French Revolution was a complex and contested process that profoundly altered French society and left a lasting legacy on global history, laying the groundwork for modern democratic institutions while exposing the challenges and contradictions inherent in revolutionary change."}}